# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules.  [source] [destination] [status]
#
# Evaluated before static assets are served, so a rule here wins over a file of
# the same name.
#
# Rules are matched top to bottom and the FIRST match wins, so anything
# specific has to sit above the catch-all at the bottom of this file.
#
# ── One URL that was never meant to exist ───────────────────────────────────
# https://jaan.io/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md/ is live, indexed,
# and an accident: a file named 2016-07-24-…-perspectives.md.bak sat in the
# Jekyll _posts directory, Jekyll's glob matched it anyway, and it published a
# page whose slug carries the .md through into the URL. What it serves is a cut
# section of the VAE tutorial — three bullets on why the probability-model view
# beats the neural-net one — with no title and no frontmatter.
#
# So it does not become a page here. It sends its traffic to the post the text
# was cut from, which is the nearest thing to what a visitor arriving on it was
# looking for. Both forms are listed rather than letting the slashed one fall
# through to the rule below, which would land it on a bare .md path that has
# nothing behind it: one hop from wherever the link points, not two into a 404.
/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md/ /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301

# ── Trailing slash → no trailing slash ──────────────────────────────────────
# The site serves /about, not /about/ (astro.config.mjs `trailingSlash: never`
# + `format: "file"`, which emits about.html). This is the other half of that
# decision: one canonical URL per page, and one permanent redirect pointing at
# it, so the same content is never reachable at two live addresses.
#
# It is not cosmetic here. jaan.io has served `permalink: /:title/` since 2013,
# so EVERY indexed URL — the ones carrying 20–30k organic visits a month — ends
# in a slash. At DNS cutover this rule is what turns each of them from a 404
# into a 301 onto the new form. Losing it would not look broken in testing (the
# nav links are already slash-less) and would quietly drop the traffic the port
# exists to keep.
#
# 301, not 302: the move is permanent, and only a 301 passes ranking signals on
# to the destination.
#
# The splat cannot swallow the site root. `/*/` requires a slash AFTER whatever
# `*` matches, and "/" has no second slash — so "/" never matches and there is
# no redirect loop. Verified against the deployment, not assumed.
/*/ /:splat 301
